    production1: 'The production record for Light Beyond Static describes an instrumental in E minor at 136 BPM, with a source duration of approximately 2 minutes 31 seconds. It was rendered locally from oscillator and seeded-noise synthesis. In this process, oscillators provide repeating waveforms and seeded noise supplies reproducible noise signals; the arrangement combines these into the track. This description applies to this release, not automatically to every video in the catalogue.',
    production2: 'The notebook records a first full drop at approximately 0:14, a breakdown at 0:42, a second build beginning around 1:11 and a second drop around 1:25. These are arrangement markers from the production data. You can use them to compare how the piece changes across sections; they are not conclusions from an independent listening review.',
    production3: 'The video’s visualizer uses the actual master audio, rather than an unrelated looping animation. Each frame analyses a short window of sound using an FFT, a method for separating a signal into frequency components. The renderer groups the result into 112 logarithmically spaced bands between 40 Hz and 16 kHz. Logarithmic spacing gives lower frequencies more room than equally spaced frequency steps would. A separate pulse is driven by measured low-frequency energy between 35 Hz and 160 Hz.',
    production4: 'The analysis window is centred on the audio sample corresponding to each frame. The image is rendered at 30 frames per second. Smoothing makes the display react and then settle instead of changing abruptly with every measurement. As you watch, consider the difference between a brief change in energy and a sustained one: a spectrum display shows energy over frequency, not the identity of every instrument or the meaning of the music.',
    production5: 'Technical checks for the source master recorded -13.96 LUFS integrated loudness, -1.66 dBTP true peak and zero clipped samples. LUFS estimates programme loudness; true peak estimates peak levels between digital samples. These figures describe a checked file, not a promise of identical loudness on every device or after a platform re-encodes the video. The checks also covered complete file decoding and audio/video timing. No independent listening evaluation is claimed.',
